[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Thursday October 6th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  the death of a 22 year old Iranian woman  Masa amini while in police custody last  month has sparked protests throughout  Iran crowds have clashed with security  forces and women have burned their  headscarves in a rejection of the  country's strict laws on women's attire  after Iran restricted internet access to  try and curb the protests the Biden  Administration and Elon Musk both  stepped forward to say they'd help  protesters stay connected but that  promise has been hard to execute on and  it's left demonstrators struggling to  keep their messages private from the  government while getting the word out to  other protesters and the rest of the  world  joining us to explain the situation is  our Middle East correspondent David  Cloud hi David thanks for joining me  good to be here David can you start by  giving us a rundown of what internet  access looks like in Iran right now in  the last two weeks the Iranian  government has imposed pretty tight  restrictions on use of the internet  particularly at night they shut down the  system and put blocks on the system and  prevent people from using email all  sorts of techniques to prevent people  from communicating particularly within  the country they don't want protesters  communicating but they also don't want  them beaming video outside the country  that people like journalists and  activists and others use in recent days  we've also seen activists and computer  Engineers arrested so it's a pretty wide  scale Crackdown on on internet use and  internet expertise now protesters are  still trying to get access to the  internet to communicate with each other  to communicate with the outside world  and talk to us a little bit about how  they're trying to do that they are and I  should say you know the internet is not  fully shut down even Iran a very close  country can't afford to do that frankly  they still need it for all sorts of  reasons many of the same reasons we  needed here you know in the west uh  economic transactions communication the  regime uses it for communication so they  can't completely shut it down but people  are worried about their safety in  communicating and in sending videos so  you know thousands of people are trying  to set up vpns and have set up vpns  virtual private networks they're using  encrypted email all right so the US  government did lift some export controls  to help Iranians get unrestricted access  to the internet and then Elon Musk  tweeted that starlink from his company  SpaceX had been activated in the country  has that made difference so far I mean  star length if it works it offers a way  to get sort of independent access to the  internet through an orbiting satellite  however it's a logistical nightmare to  make it work you got to get the  terminals into Iran which you know  hasn't happened on a very large scale  you have to get the terminals linked to  the satellite it all has to work and  there's also the possibility that the  regime will jam it I mean there's no  doubt that they have the capability of  finding these signals and jamming them  you spoke with some Iranians trying to  set up a starlink connection can you  tell us about their experience one of  them is a computer engineer the other  one his identity is unknown to me so  this account comes from the computer  engineer they obtained two starlink  terminals from Dubai which is right  across the Persian Gulf from Iran and  had been trying even before the protests  broke out to connect to the satellites  and had not been able to do so when the  protest broke out and they heard Elon  musk's assertion that that starlink was  now activated in Iran they got quite  excited and optimistic and went onto the  roof of a house in an Iranian City and  again attempted to connect the Starling  terminal to the satellite what I was  told was they got a signal very briefly  for a few seconds from the satellite  then it disappeared so they haven't been  able to get access to the internet using  starlink has SpaceX commented on that  starlink has not uh commented nor has  SpaceX we sent them detailed questions  about what we heard from this Iranian  computer engineer and about his  experience and they didn't respond to  comment and I want to take a second to  focus on SpaceX we saw them provide  internet access in Ukraine during the  war how important are these crises to  spacex's efforts to get starlink you  know more widely used  I don't think it's Central to their  business model at all they say have said  they have around 400 000 users worldwide  I would expect these are Travelers these  are people who go to rural areas and  need access to the internet  I don't think the system is designed for  conflict zones particularly not conflict  zones like Iran which have repressive  governments and which you know tightly  control access to the internet it was  used in Ukraine after Russia invaded But  Ukraine's government wanted starlink  they wanted to be able to sustain  internet access for it for people so  what comes next then for the protesters  who are trying to get internet access to  spread their message outside of Iran  what comes next I mean I think they just  have to keep trying and keep working at  it it's not going to be easy in some  ways  internet access is not the critical  thing I mean yes it's nice to know  what's going on there and it's important  to know what's going on there obviously  it helps the protests gain International  support it helps sustain their momentum  I suppose but no I think they're going  to have to use the techniques that they  have been using and to try to find more  ways to do it I mean VPN networks using  access to the internet when it's  available and sending out videos Etc I  mean there aren't great options unless  something like starlink you know becomes  widely available  all right we'll have to leave it there  David Cloud wsj Middle East  correspondent thank you so much for  joining us my pleasure  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal thanks for listening  [Music]

